# Agentic Cinema: The Blockbuster Hackathon — Idea Deck<br><br>**25 USP-driven agent ideas — 5 per partner track.**<br><br>> **Deadline:** September 7, 2026 · 2:00 PM PDT<br>> **Prize:** $75,000 total — each track pays $7,500 / $4,500 / $3,000 (top 3)<br>> **Base requirement (ALL tracks):** A functional autonomous agent powered by **Google Gemini + Google Cloud Agent Builder (Vertex AI / ADK)**, integrating the partner's product/MCP **at runtime** (real code path), solving a real **Media & Entertainment** workflow, shipped with a public open-source GitHub repo + a 3-minute demo video.<br><br>---<br><br>## Contents<br><br>| Track | Requirement (in brief) | Ideas |<br>|-------|------------------------|-------|<br>| [🔵 IBM](#-ibm-track) | Built with **IBM Bob** during dev · Confluent (Kafka/Flink) streaming · **watsonx.governance** audit | ControlRoom Copilot · StreamWarden · RoyaltyRoute · GuildKeeper · RenderMarshal |<br>| [🟠 Grafana](#-grafana-track) | Connect to **grafana/mcp-grafana** MCP at runtime (read metrics/logs/traces + write dashboards/alerts) | ShowRunner · RenderWrangler · MatchKeeper · AdSentinel · DropWatch |<br>| [🟢 Parallel](#-parallel-track) | **Parallel Search/Task/Extract/Monitor/FindAll** APIs at runtime via `parallel-web` SDK | VeritasCut · ClearVault · CastGuard · WindowWar · LoreKeeper |<br>| [🟡 ClickHouse](#-clickhouse-track) | **mcp-clickhouse** at runtime — text-to-SQL over billions of event rows | ChurnCircuit · AdReconAgent · RenderReaper · GuildSignal · HookRadar |<br>| [🔴 Replit](#-replit-track) | Built with **Replit Agent** AND deployed on a live `replit.app`/`replit.dev` URL | PremiereForge · QuestDrop · PlotBranch · SplitReel · DailiesDrop |<br><br>---<br><br>## 🔵 IBM Track<br><br>*Built with IBM Bob during development · Confluent (`mcp-confluent`: `produce-message`, `consume-messages`, `create-flink-statement`, `get-flink-statement-results`, `search-topics-by-tag`, `create-schema`, `query-metrics`, `add-tags-to-topic`) strongly encouraged · **watsonx.governance** as the load-bearing enterprise audit/governance runtime.*<br><br>### 1. ControlRoom Copilot — *live broadcast production*<br>**One-liner:** An autonomous production assistant that watches every live-broadcast signal on Kafka, invents its own Flink join to score dramatic "moments," and proposes camera cuts + auto-highlights in real time.<br>**M&E problem:** Live directors (sports, awards, news) manually watch 20+ camera/audio/stats feeds and miss peak moments; TDs feel this pressure most, and lost highlights = lost social/clip revenue.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons over the semantic shape of the incoming signal topics (camera metadata, crowd audio dB, play-by-play stats, social velocity) and decides which streams correlate to "drama." Agent Builder orchestrates sub-agents: a Topology sub-agent (discovers topics), a MomentScorer (authors + deploys Flink SQL), and a Director sub-agent (turns scores into cut proposals). Autonomous loop: observe → score → propose → write decision → learn from operator accept/reject.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `search-topics-by-tag` to discover live-production topics; `create-schema` for a `moment.score` Avro schema; `create-flink-statement` to deploy a self-authored streaming join across camera/audio/stats topics; `get-flink-statement-results` to poll scored windows; `produce-message` to write cut decisions to a `director.cuts` topic consumed by the switcher. Every cut decision + the Flink SQL that produced it is logged to **watsonx.governance** as an auditable model-decision record.<br>**USP:** The agent writes and deploys its *own* Flink SQL at runtime rather than running a fixed pipeline — the streaming topology is emergent, not hardcoded, and fully governed/replayable.<br>**Demoability:** Split-screen of a mock live feed; the agent narrates why it's cutting to camera 3, the auto-generated Flink SQL appears, a highlight clip pops out, and the watsonx.governance audit entry is shown live.<br><br>### 2. StreamWarden — *OTT compliance & localization gatekeeper*<br>**One-liner:** A per-segment compliance agent that inspects every FAST-channel/OTT stream slice against territory rules and autonomously issues remediations before non-compliant content reaches a viewer.<br>**M&E problem:** Streamers running dozens of FAST channels across regions risk fines for wrong ad insertion, missing subtitles, or forbidden content per territory; compliance and ad-ops teams feel it, and fines map to specific broadcast moments they can't prove after the fact.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons about each segment's manifest/metadata vs. a territory rulebook (ratings, ad rules, language mandates). Agent Builder runs a Consumer sub-agent (per-channel), an Enricher (Flink), and a Remediator that decides packager vs. ad-server fixes. Loop: consume segment → enrich + evaluate → remediate → prove.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `consume-messages` on per-channel segment topics; `create-flink-statement` to enrich each segment against territory-rule reference data; `produce-message` to push remediation commands to packager/ad-server topics; `add-tags-to-topic` to drive Confluent Stream Lineage so every segment's journey is traceable. **watsonx.governance** stores a decision log that maps each fine-risk verdict to the exact Kafka offset/segment, giving auditors a moment-precise compliance trail.<br>**USP:** Fine-risk is bound to an immutable Kafka offset via Stream Lineage + governance — turning "we think we were compliant" into cryptographic, replayable per-segment proof.<br>**Demoability:** A dashboard of live FAST segments; the agent flags a segment missing a mandated subtitle track, fires a remediation to the packager, and the watsonx.governance log resolves the fine-risk down to the precise offset.<br><br>### 3. RoyaltyRoute — *music streaming rights & royalty reconciliation*<br>**One-liner:** A real-time royalty-split agent that reconciles every music play-event against layered rights ownership, catches split conflicts the instant they happen, and produces an auditable payout ledger.<br>**M&E problem:** Music streaming royalties are reconciled in monthly batches, so ownership conflicts (overlapping publisher claims, unregistered samples, disputed splits) surface weeks late and trigger costly clawbacks; label finance teams, publishers, and DSP rights-ops feel the pain, and "black box" royalty statements erode artist trust.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons over play-event streams joined against a rights graph (master owner, publishers, writers, sample interpolations) and detects when claimed splits exceed 100%, contradict each other, or reference an unregistered work. Agent Builder orchestrates an Ingest sub-agent, a ConflictReasoner (Gemini interprets ambiguous/overlapping claims), and a Ledger sub-agent. Loop: stream plays → aggregate accruals → detect conflict → quarantine + escalate → post clean payouts.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `create-schema` for a `royalty.accrual` schema; `create-flink-statement` to deploy a windowed streaming aggregation that sums per-rightsholder accruals and computes running split percentages per ISRC; `get-flink-statement-results` to surface any track whose total split ≠ 100%; `produce-message` to push conflicts to a `rights.disputes` topic and clean accruals to `payout.ledger`; `query-metrics` to monitor accrual throughput/lag as an SLA. Each payout or quarantine decision — with the split math and rights evidence Gemini used — is recorded in **watsonx.governance** so every cent paid is explainable and dispute-defensible.<br>**USP:** Turns royalty accounting from an opaque monthly batch into a live, self-auditing stream where conflicts are caught at the moment of play and every payout carries a governance-grade explanation — defensible in a rights dispute.<br>**Demoability:** A simulated play-event firehose; the agent lights up a track whose publisher splits total 112%, quarantines it to the disputes topic while clean tracks post to the ledger, and shows the watsonx.governance record explaining exactly why one artist's payout was held.<br><br>### 4. GuildKeeper — *gaming & live-stream community safety + brand safety*<br>**One-liner:** An always-on moderation agent for live game streams that fuses chat, voice-transcript, and gameplay telemetry to enforce creator safety and advertiser brand-safety rules in real time, with a governed record of every enforcement action.<br>**M&E problem:** Live-streaming platforms and esports orgs must moderate millions of concurrent chat/voice messages; toxic spikes and brand-unsafe moments (slurs during a sponsored segment, doxxing, cheat-promotion) cause advertiser pullouts and Trust & Safety liability, and human mods can't keep pace or justify bans after appeals.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons over correlated streams — chat velocity, voice-to-text, and gameplay telemetry (e.g., a suspicious kill-pattern hinting at a cheat being promoted) — to classify severity and intent, distinguishing banter from genuine harm in context. Agent Builder orchestrates a Chat sub-agent, a VoiceTranscript sub-agent, a ContextFuser, and an Enforcer that chooses timeout/ban/ad-pause. Loop: ingest multi-modal streams → contextual classify → enforce proportionally → log + enable appeal.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `consume-messages` on `chat.messages` and `voice.transcripts` topics per channel; `create-flink-statement` to join chat/voice with a `gameplay.telemetry` stream and compute a rolling per-stream toxicity + brand-safety score; `add-tags-to-topic` to tag streams entering "brand-unsafe" state so ad-ops can pause monetization; `produce-message` to emit enforcement actions to `moderation.actions` and an ad-pause signal to the ad server. Every ban/timeout/ad-pause decision, with the multi-modal evidence and Gemini's severity rationale, is written to **watsonx.governance** as the system of record for appeals and advertiser reporting.<br>**USP:** Context-fusion across chat + voice + *gameplay telemetry* (not text-only moderation) means it catches cheat-promotion and coordinated harassment invisible to chat filters — and every action is appeal-ready and advertiser-auditable via governance.<br>**Demoability:** A mock stream where chat looks fine but the voice transcript + a telemetry anomaly reveal cheat-selling; the agent auto-pauses the sponsor's ad, times out the offender, tags the topic brand-unsafe, and displays the watsonx.governance appeal record.<br><br>### 5. RenderMarshal — *VFX & post-production render-farm orchestration*<br>**One-liner:** An autonomous render-farm dispatcher that streams live node telemetry, reallocates VFX jobs to hit shot deadlines and budget, and enforces asset-provenance/IP compliance on every frame it green-lights.<br>**M&E problem:** VFX studios run thousands of render jobs against hard delivery dates; nodes fail silently, costs overrun, and — critically — a shot can be rendered with an unlicensed asset or leaked pre-release IP, which is a contractual and legal disaster. Render wranglers, VFX supes, and studio security all feel it, and post-mortems can't reconstruct which asset went into which frame.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons over node health, queue depth, per-shot deadline slack, and asset-license metadata to decide job priority and placement, and to *refuse* jobs referencing unlicensed or embargoed assets. Agent Builder orchestrates a Telemetry sub-agent, a Scheduler (cost/SLA optimizer), and a Provenance sub-agent (Gemini validates asset manifests against license terms). Loop: observe farm + queue → prioritize/reallocate → provenance-gate → dispatch → prove per-frame lineage.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `consume-messages` on `render.node.telemetry` and `render.queue` topics; `create-flink-statement` to compute a live cost-burn + deadline-risk score per shot and detect stalled/failing nodes; `query-metrics` to watch consumer lag as a farm-saturation signal; `produce-message` to dispatch reprioritized jobs to `render.dispatch` and provenance-rejections to `render.blocked`; `add-tags-to-topic` to attach Stream Lineage so every frame traces back to its source assets. **watsonx.governance** logs each dispatch/rejection with the asset-license reasoning, producing a per-frame IP-provenance ledger that survives a legal audit.<br>**USP:** It fuses cost/SLA scheduling with runtime IP-provenance gating in one governed loop — most render managers optimize throughput but can't *prove* no unlicensed or embargoed asset touched a delivered frame; RenderMarshal makes that a streaming, auditable guarantee.<br>**Demoability:** A live farm dashboard where a node stalls and the agent reroutes jobs to protect a deadline, then it hard-blocks a shot referencing an unlicensed stock asset, tags the lineage, and shows the watsonx.governance per-frame provenance record proving the block.<br><br>---<br><br>## 🟠 Grafana Track<br><br>*Connect to **grafana/mcp-grafana** MCP at runtime (60+ tools: `query_prometheus`, `query_prometheus_histogram`, `query_loki_logs`, `query_loki_patterns`, `find_error_pattern_logs` / `find_slow_requests` (Sift), `query_pyroscope`, `search_dashboards`, `get_dashboard_by_uid`, `update_dashboard`, `get_panel_image`, `alerting_manage_rules`, `create_incident`, `add_activity_to_incident`, `get_current_oncall_users`). The agent must **read** metrics/logs/traces AND **write** dashboards/alerts.*<br><br>### 1. ShowRunner — *live-stream QoE war-room (OTT/streaming)*<br>**One-liner:** An autonomous SRE-director that watches a live broadcast's quality-of-experience and reshapes its own dashboards and alerts as the audience swells.<br>**M&E problem:** During tent-pole live streams (finales, sports, concerts), rebuffering and startup-latency spikes cause viewer churn in seconds; NOC engineers can't hand-tune dashboards and alert thresholds fast enough as concurrency 10x's mid-event.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini continuously reasons over a QoE hypothesis tree (CDN edge vs. origin vs. encoder vs. player), Agent Builder orchestrates sub-agents for metrics, logs, and remediation. The loop: pull concurrency + rebuffer ratio, localize the failing tier, mutate the war-room board to foreground the culprit, then rescale alert thresholds proportional to live concurrency so pages fire on rate-of-change, not stale static limits.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `query_prometheus` (join_concurrent_viewers, rebuffer_ratio), `query_prometheus_histogram` (startup latency P95/P99), `query_loki_patterns` on CDN edge logs to cluster 5xx surges by POP, `find_slow_requests` (Sift) to localize origin fetch stalls, `update_dashboard` to rebuild panels around the implicated tier, `alerting_manage_rules` to set concurrency-scaled thresholds, `create_incident` + `get_current_oncall_users` to page the right rotation.<br>**USP:** The dashboard is a live character that rewrites itself around the fault — self-authoring observability tuned to audience size, not a static board a human stares at.<br>**Demoability:** Video shows a simulated concurrency ramp; rebuffer spikes; the agent narrates its hypothesis, the board visibly reflows to the CDN tier, an alert rule updates to the new concurrency, and an incident opens with the on-call named.<br><br>### 2. RenderWrangler — *VFX render-farm ops (post-production/VFX)*<br>**One-liner:** An overnight farm foreman that fuses render cost, delivery schedule, and frame correctness, and hunts the plugin that's silently leaking memory.<br>**M&E problem:** VFX shops run thousands of nightly render jobs against hard client delivery dates; a single leaking plugin or misbudgeted queue can blow the schedule and burn cloud spend, discovered only at morning standup when it's too late.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons over a delivery-risk model (frames remaining × per-frame cost × failure rate vs. deadline). Agent Builder runs sub-agents for profiling, cost, and scheduling. The loop: profile worker processes, correlate RSS growth to a specific plugin/DSO, project completion time against the deadline, and stand up a delivery-risk board plus cost-burn alerts before humans wake.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `query_pyroscope` to diff allocation/CPU profiles across render workers and pin the leaking plugin frame, `query_prometheus` for queue depth, per-frame $ and GPU utilization, `find_error_pattern_logs` on farm scheduler logs for retry storms, `update_dashboard` to publish a delivery-risk board (ETA vs. deadline, burn rate), `alerting_manage_rules` for cost-burn thresholds, `create_incident` + `add_activity_to_incident` to log the offending plugin and remediation.<br>**USP:** Pyroscope profiling turned into a business artifact — it ties a flamegraph regression directly to "shot 042 misses the client deadline and costs $X," a fusion of profiler and producer.<br>**Demoability:** Video shows a seeded memory-leak plugin; the agent diffs flamegraphs, names the plugin, projects the schedule slip, publishes the risk board, and fires a cost-burn alert with an incident note.<br><br>### 3. MatchKeeper — *game-launch live-ops (gaming)*<br>**One-liner:** A day-one launch commander that guards matchmaking fairness and server tick-rate as players flood in, quarantining bad server builds before the community revolts.<br>**M&E problem:** Multiplayer launch day is the make-or-break M&E event — matchmaking queue explosions, crash-looping game servers, and degraded tick-rate produce viral rage clips within minutes, and live-ops teams triage blind across hundreds of regional server fleets.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons over a per-region fleet health matrix (queue time × tick-rate × crash rate × build version), Agent Builder spawns sub-agents for matchmaking, server-health, and build-blame. The autonomous loop: detect a region where P95 queue time diverges, correlate to a tick-rate/crash regression, blame it on a specific server build hash via log patterns, then raise a build-quarantine alert and reflow the launch board to that region + build. It scales queue-time SLOs by live player count so alerts track the ramp.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `query_prometheus_histogram` for matchmaking queue-time percentiles per region, `query_prometheus` for tick-rate and CCU, `query_loki_patterns` to cluster crash stack signatures and extract the failing build hash, `find_error_pattern_logs` (Sift) for anomalous crash bursts, `query_pyroscope` on game-server processes to catch a physics/netcode CPU regression in the bad build, `search_dashboards` + `get_dashboard_by_uid` to find the fleet board and `update_dashboard` to pin the bad-build region, `alerting_manage_rules` for CCU-scaled queue SLOs, `create_incident` + `get_current_oncall_users` to page the fleet owner.<br>**USP:** It performs build-blame — mapping a live gameplay regression to the exact server build hash and quarantining it — turning observability into an automated launch-day release gate, not just a monitor.<br>**Demoability:** Video simulates a CCU ramp with one bad build seeded into a region; the agent isolates that region, names the build hash from crash-log patterns, shows the Pyroscope netcode regression, quarantines the build via alert, and reflows the launch board.<br><br>### 4. AdSentinel — *programmatic ad revenue integrity (advertising / SSAI)*<br>**One-liner:** A revenue-guardian agent that watches server-side ad insertion during live breaks and converts VAST/beacon failures into dollars-lost the moment fill rate cracks.<br>**M&E problem:** In ad-supported streaming, a single SSAI stitching bug or ad-server timeout during a high-CPM live sports break silently drops fill rate and torches millions in ad revenue — and ad-ops learns about it days later in a reconciliation report, not live.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons over an ad-revenue integrity model (impressions expected × CPM × fill-rate delta = revenue-at-risk). Agent Builder orchestrates sub-agents for ad-decisioning telemetry, VAST error taxonomy, and revenue math. The loop: on an ad break, watch fill rate and beacon-fire ratio, cluster VAST error codes to a specific creative/ad-partner/SSAI stitcher, compute live revenue-at-risk, render a snapshot for the ad-ops exec, and set revenue-loss-per-minute alerts scoped to the current break's CPM.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `query_prometheus` for fill rate, ad-request QPS and impression-beacon fire ratio, `query_loki_logs` + `query_loki_patterns` to bucket VAST/VPAID error codes (303/400 timeout, empty-VAST) by ad partner and creative ID, `find_slow_requests` (Sift) to catch ad-decision server latency stalling the stitcher, `update_dashboard` to build a live revenue-at-risk board (CPM × missed impressions), `get_panel_image` to attach a revenue-loss snapshot into `create_incident` / `add_activity_to_incident` for the ad-ops bridge, `alerting_manage_rules` to set $-per-minute loss thresholds tuned to the break's CPM.<br>**USP:** It speaks money, not metrics — translating raw VAST error patterns into live "we're losing $/min from Partner X's creative," and it's the rare agent that images a panel into the incident so execs see the revenue graph, not a metric name.<br>**Demoability:** Video plays a live break; the agent detects fill-rate collapse, clusters the VAST error to one ad partner, shows the auto-built revenue-at-risk board with a dollar counter, and opens an incident with the panel image embedded.<br><br>### 5. DropWatch — *music release-day midnight drop (music streaming)*<br>**One-liner:** A midnight-release conductor that shields a headline album drop from cache-miss storms and royalty-log gaps so every first-hour play is served fast and counted.<br>**M&E problem:** When a superstar album drops at 00:00, demand hammers cold CDN caches for brand-new audio segments while play-count/royalty events must be logged perfectly — cache-miss storms cause playback stalls and, worse, dropped play events under-report streams, corrupting royalty payouts and chart eligibility.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons over a dual-objective model: playback QoE (cache-hit ratio, audio startup) and royalty-log integrity (play-start vs. play-logged reconciliation). Agent Builder runs sub-agents for CDN warmth, audio delivery, and royalty-ledger reconciliation. The loop at T-minus: pre-warm-verify cache-hit ratio for the new release's asset IDs, detect cold-POP miss storms, and reconcile emitted play-start events against logged royalty events — flagging a per-track under-count, publishing a drop-day board, and alerting on any royalty-log lag that threatens chart cutoffs.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `query_prometheus` for cache-hit ratio scoped to the release's asset IDs and play-events-per-second, `query_prometheus_histogram` for audio startup latency, `query_loki_patterns` to detect cold-POP cache-miss bursts and cluster royalty-pipeline dead-letter/drop log lines, `find_error_pattern_logs` (Sift) on the royalty ingestion service for silent event drops, `update_dashboard` to build a "midnight drop" board (per-track hit ratio + play-start-vs-logged delta), `alerting_manage_rules` to alert when logged-play lag exceeds the chart-reporting window, `create_incident` + `get_current_oncall_users` to page the royalty-pipeline owner with the reconciliation gap.<br>**USP:** It defends both the ear and the ledger — uniquely treating royalty-log integrity (streams counted = artists paid = charts correct) as a first-class SLO alongside playback, a correctness dimension no generic uptime monitor watches.<br>**Demoability:** Video shows a T-0 drop with a seeded cold-POP miss storm and a royalty-log dropper; the agent flags the miss storm, surfaces a per-track play-start-vs-logged undercount, builds the drop-day board, and opens an incident on the royalty gap before the chart window closes.<br><br>---<br><br>## 🟢 Parallel Track<br><br>*Integrate **Parallel Search / Task / Extract / Monitor / FindAll** APIs at runtime via the `parallel-web` SDK. Key surface: `client.search(objective, search_queries, mode, max_results)` → `results[].{url,title,excerpts,publish_date}`; `client.task_run.create(input, processor, task_spec.output_schema)` with per-field `result.output.basis[]` citations; `client.extract(urls, objective)`; `client.monitor.create(type, frequency, settings, webhook)`; `client.beta.findall.entity_search(entity_type, objective, match_limit)`.*<br><br>### 1. VeritasCut — *documentary / post-production fact-checking*<br>**One-liner:** An autonomous claim auditor that watches a documentary cut and returns an insurance-grade, timecode-anchored dossier proving or flagging every factual assertion on screen.<br>**M&E problem:** E&O (errors & omissions) insurers and network standards-and-practices teams require every factual claim in a documentary to be sourced before airing; today a human researcher spends weeks manually verifying claims against primary sources, and one missed falsehood can kill a distribution deal or trigger litigation.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini ingests the transcript + timecodes, segments it into atomic verifiable claims, and classifies each by type (statistic, quote, date, causal assertion). Agent Builder spawns one verification sub-agent per claim in parallel; each reasons about what evidence would confirm/refute it, gathers sources, and returns a verdict with confidence. A supervisor agent reconciles verdicts into a single dossier keyed to timecodes.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** Each sub-agent calls `client.search(objective, search_queries, mode="pro")` for candidate sources, then `client.extract(urls, objective)` to pull the exact supporting passage from primary documents. The verdict itself is produced by `client.task_run.create(input, processor="pro", task_spec.output_schema={claim, verdict, confidence, sources})` so every field arrives with `result.output.basis[]` citations, which map directly into the timecode dossier as court-defensible evidence.<br>**USP:** Not a chatbot fact-check — it produces a structured, per-field-cited legal artifact (the `basis[]` array becomes the audit trail) that mirrors exactly what E&O underwriters demand. Parallelism = one agent per claim, so a 90-min film audits in minutes.<br>**Demoability:** Play a documentary clip; the dossier populates live — green/amber/red chips snap onto the timeline, click a red flag to reveal the disputing source excerpt and citation URL pulled by Parallel.<br><br>### 2. ClearVault — *pre-production IP & rights clearance*<br>**One-liner:** A pre-production clearance scout that autonomously hunts for prior use, trademark/title collisions, and rights holders before a name, logo, or concept enters the script.<br>**M&E problem:** Studio legal and clearance departments must confirm that a fictional brand, character name, song, or product doesn't collide with a real trademark or prior work; missed collisions cause reshoots, blurred logos, or lawsuits discovered mid-production at enormous cost.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini parses the script/pitch bible and extracts every clearable entity (names, marks, businesses, songs, likenesses). Agent Builder orchestrates a high-recall enumeration pass, then a confirmation pass, then a standing-watch pass. Gemini reasons about collision risk severity and assembles a clearance memo per entity.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `client.beta.findall.entity_search(entity_type, objective, match_limit)` does high-recall collision enumeration (find every real business/product matching the fictional name). `client.search()` establishes prior-use timelines. `client.task_run.create(...)` confirms the actual rights-holder with `basis[]` citations. Finally `client.monitor.create(type, frequency, settings, webhook)` sets clearance-drift alerts so a new trademark filing during a long production triggers a webhook.<br>**USP:** FindAll's recall-first enumeration is the defensible core — clearance failures are recall problems (the one collision you missed), and no generic search matches entity_search's exhaustive coverage. Plus continuous monitoring turns a one-time check into living clearance.<br>**Demoability:** Drop in a script page; entities light up with risk scores, expand a "HIGH" to see the real colliding trademark FindAll surfaced, then trigger a mock new-filing webhook and watch a drift alert fire.<br><br>### 3. CastGuard — *advertising / creator & talent brand-safety vetting*<br>**One-liner:** An autonomous brand-safety vetting agent that, given a campaign brief, enumerates candidate creators/talent and returns a cited risk-and-fit scorecard for each before any contract is signed.<br>**M&E problem:** Brand marketers and talent agencies must vet influencers and on-camera talent for past controversies, off-brand affiliations, and audience-fit before a sponsorship; a single overlooked old post or undisclosed competitor deal becomes a PR crisis and a clawback lawsuit. Manual vetting is slow, shallow, and misses buried history.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reads the campaign brief (brand values, target demo, red-line topics) and defines the candidate profile. Agent Builder runs a discovery pass to build a candidate pool, then a per-candidate deep-dive sub-agent that reasons about each creator's content history, controversy exposure, competitor conflicts, and audience alignment, scoring against the brand's specific red lines.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `client.beta.findall.entity_search(entity_type="creator/person", objective)` enumerates candidates matching the campaign's niche at high recall. For each, `client.search(objective, search_queries, mode="pro")` surfaces news/controversy/interviews and `client.extract(urls, objective)` pulls the damning or reassuring passages. `client.task_run.create(..., output_schema={brand_fit, controversy_risk, competitor_conflicts, verdict})` produces the scorecard with per-field `result.output.basis[]` citations so every risk flag links to its source. `client.monitor.create(...)` keeps a watch on signed talent for the campaign's duration, webhook-alerting on emerging controversy.<br>**USP:** The `basis[]` citations turn "gut feeling" vetting into an auditable due-diligence record legal can stand behind, and post-signing monitoring catches the controversy that erupts *after* the deal — the exact gap that torpedoes campaigns. Recall-first discovery means it also *finds* the ideal under-the-radar creator, not just vets a given name.<br>**Demoability:** Enter a brand brief; a ranked creator grid populates with fit/risk bars, open a low-scorer to see the old controversial post Parallel extracted with its citation, then fire a mock monitor webhook showing a "signed talent — new risk detected" alert.<br><br>### 4. WindowWar — *streaming distribution & release-window strategy*<br>**One-liner:** A release-window war-room agent that continuously scouts competitor release calendars and market signals to recommend the optimal launch date and counter-programming play for a title.<br>**M&E problem:** Streaming and theatrical distribution teams gamble enormous P&A budgets on release dates; launching against a rival tentpole or in a saturated genre window buries a title, and competitor date-shifts (constant in the industry) invalidate plans overnight. Tracking the moving competitive calendar is a manual, always-stale spreadsheet.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini models the title's genre, audience, and comps, then reasons about crowding and counter-programming windows. Agent Builder orchestrates a competitive-calendar builder, a market-signal analyzer (comparable-title performance, festival timing, seasonality), and a recommendation synthesizer that outputs ranked date options with rationale — and re-runs autonomously whenever the landscape shifts.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `client.search(objective, search_queries, mode="pro")` gathers announced release dates, box-office/viewership comps, and genre-glut signals across trade press. `client.task_run.create(..., output_schema={recommended_windows[], crowding_score, counterprog_rationale})` synthesizes the strategy with `basis[]` citations grounding each competitive claim (so a VP can trust "avoid this weekend"). Critically, `client.monitor.create(type="competitive_release", frequency, settings, webhook)` watches rival studios' announcements — when a competitor shifts a tentpole into your window, the webhook fires and the agent auto-re-runs, pushing a revised recommendation.<br>**USP:** It's a *living* release-strategy engine, not a one-shot report — the monitor→webhook→re-plan loop mirrors how the industry actually behaves (dates move constantly), and every recommendation is citation-grounded in real trade intelligence rather than vibes.<br>**Demoability:** Load a title; a calendar heatmap shows crowded vs. open windows with a top recommendation and its cited reasoning, then inject a mock "competitor moved their blockbuster to your date" event and watch the monitor fire and the recommendation visibly re-rank.<br><br>### 5. LoreKeeper — *franchise continuity / TV writers' room & publishing*<br>**One-liner:** A continuity-bible agent that autonomously builds and guards a franchise's canon, flagging every contradiction between a new script and decades of established lore — with a source citation for each conflict.<br>**M&E problem:** Long-running franchises (TV universes, comic lines, game series, book series) accumulate sprawling canon across dozens of installments; writers' rooms and franchise editors constantly introduce continuity errors (a character's established backstory, a timeline, a rule of the world), and fans mercilessly catch them — damaging brand credibility. Maintaining the "bible" by hand is impossible at franchise scale.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reads a new script/manuscript and extracts every canonical assertion it makes (character facts, timeline events, world rules). Agent Builder orchestrates a canon-corpus builder that assembles the established lore, then a per-assertion continuity checker sub-agent that reasons about whether the new claim contradicts, extends, or is silent on prior canon, and a synthesizer that produces a continuity report ranked by severity.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `client.beta.findall.entity_search(entity_type, objective)` enumerates all canonical entities (characters, locations, artifacts) across the franchise's published/wiki footprint at high recall. `client.extract(urls, objective)` pulls the precise established-canon passages from official wikis, published works, and reference sources into a structured lore store. Each continuity check runs `client.task_run.create(..., output_schema={assertion, canon_status, contradiction_detail, canonical_source})` so every flagged conflict carries a `result.output.basis[]` citation pointing to the exact prior installment that establishes the truth. `client.monitor.create(...)` watches for newly released franchise material so the canon store stays current.<br>**USP:** Continuity is fundamentally a recall + grounding problem — you must find *the one* prior fact that contradicts, and prove it — which is exactly FindAll's exhaustive enumeration plus per-field `basis[]` citations. No generic LLM can cite "Season 3, Episode 7 established X"; this agent does, turning a fandom-gotcha risk into a defensible pre-air safeguard.<br>**Demoability:** Paste a new script page; continuity flags appear inline, click a red "CANON CONFLICT" to reveal the contradicting established fact and the exact citation Parallel extracted, and show the FindAll-built entity list of the franchise's characters that seeded the canon store.<br><br>---<br><br>## 🟡 ClickHouse Track<br><br>*Use **mcp-clickhouse** at runtime — tools: `list_databases`, `list_tables` (with `like`/`not_like` + column metadata), `run_query` (read-only SQL over ClickHouse Cloud/self-hosted), `run_chdb_select_query` (embedded chDB over external files/URLs for no-ETL joins). Exploit ClickHouse-native functions: `windowFunnel`, `sequenceMatch`, `retention`, `uniqExact`, `topK`, `quantile`. All ideas use autonomous text-to-SQL + iterative drill-down.*<br><br>### 1. ChurnCircuit — *SVOD streaming retention*<br>**One-liner:** An autonomous retention analyst that reasons from a raw event schema to a causal explanation of *why* subscribers churned, then drafts the win-back play.<br>**M&E problem:** Streaming growth/retention teams sit on billions of playback events but can't answer "why did Q3 churn spike in the 25-34 cohort?" without a week of analyst SQL. Churn is expensive and the causal signal is buried in session sequences.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini first calls `list_databases`/`list_tables` (with column metadata) to ground itself in the actual schema — no hardcoded table names. It forms a churn hypothesis, emits text-to-SQL, runs it via `run_query`, reads the result, and iteratively drills: cohort → content-gap → session-friction. Agent Builder orchestrates a hypothesis sub-agent, a SQL-writer/validator sub-agent, and a narrative sub-agent in a loop that stops when confidence converges.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** Load-bearing `run_query` using ClickHouse-native `retention()` for cohort survival, `windowFunnel(7 DAY)(ts, watched, browsed_no_play, cancelled)` to detect the browse-abandon-cancel path, and `sequenceMatch('(?1)(?2)')` for rage-churn signatures. `run_chdb_select_query` joins internal churn timestamps against an *external* content-release calendar CSV/Parquet (no ETL) to prove "churn clustered the week after the tentpole finale dropped."<br>**USP:** It doesn't just report a churn number — it produces a *causal chain* grounded in ClickHouse funnel/retention primitives and correlates it to content supply via zero-ETL chDB join. The schema-introspection-first design means it works on any streamer's warehouse unmodified.<br>**Demoability:** Type "explain the July churn spike." Watch it list tables, fire 4-5 escalating queries live, render a retention curve, then surface "finale gap → -18% W2 retention" and auto-draft a win-back email segment.<br><br>### 2. AdReconAgent — *CTV ad-ops forensics*<br>**One-liner:** A forensic ad-fraud investigator that builds an evidence tree over CTV impression logs and files a make-good/refund case with reconciled numbers.<br>**M&E problem:** CTV/streaming ad-ops and revenue-assurance teams lose real money to IVT (invalid traffic), duplicate impressions, and SSP/DSP count discrepancies. Manual reconciliation across log providers is slow and disputes stall without hard evidence.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini runs an investigation *tree*: anomaly detection → suspect isolation → attribution. It introspects the impression schema, then autonomously widens/narrows GROUP BYs to chase outliers. Agent Builder coordinates a detector sub-agent, a forensics SQL sub-agent, and a case-file-writer, looping until the fraud pattern is isolated with a dollar impact.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `run_query` with `uniqExact(device_id)` vs raw impression counts to expose duplication, `topK(20)(ip_asn)` to surface bot data-center ASNs, and high-cardinality `GROUP BY ip, ua, bundle_id HAVING count() > threshold` to fingerprint fraud farms. `run_chdb_select_query` reconciles internal ClickHouse impression counts against *external* SSP/DSP delivery logs (S3 Parquet/CSV) in one query to quantify the exact discrepancy — the refund number.<br>**USP:** Produces a defensible, numbers-backed dispute case file, not a dashboard. The chDB internal-vs-external reconciliation in a single query is the killer move — it turns "we think we were overbilled" into "you owe us $Xk, here's the ASN evidence."<br>**Demoability:** Prompt "audit yesterday's CTV delivery." It runs the forensic tree on screen, flags a suspicious ASN with 40x duplication, reconciles against the external SSP log, and outputs a formatted make-good case file with the refund total.<br><br>### 3. RenderReaper — *VFX / post-production render farm*<br>**One-liner:** An autonomous render-farm cost & bottleneck analyst that turns billions of frame-render telemetry rows into "kill these shots, they're bleeding GPU-hours before the delivery deadline."<br>**M&E problem:** VFX houses and post shops run render farms where a single feature emits hundreds of millions of per-frame/per-node telemetry events. Producers can't see which shots, artists, or scene-complexity classes are burning render budget and threatening delivery until the invoice or the missed date arrives. Wasted GPU-hours = margin evaporating on fixed-bid contracts.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini introspects the render-log schema, then reasons about a cost/throughput hypothesis and drills: which show → which shot → which node/GPU class → which frame-complexity band is the outlier. It autonomously escalates from farm-wide aggregates to per-shot forensics, and flags retry storms (a shot re-rendering itself to death). Agent Builder runs a bottleneck-detector sub-agent, a SQL sub-agent, and a producer-report writer in a converging loop.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** `run_query` using `quantile(0.95)(frame_render_seconds)` per shot to find long-tail hogs, `topK(15)(shot_id)` on total GPU-seconds, `sum(gpu_seconds) GROUP BY node_gpu_type` for hardware-tier efficiency, and `sequenceMatch('(?1){3,}')` over `(frame_id, status)` to detect retry/crash loops that quietly triple cost. `run_chdb_select_query` joins live farm telemetry against the *external* shot/asset manifest + bid budget spreadsheet (Parquet/CSV exported from the production tracker) with zero ETL to compute "actual GPU-hours vs bid allowance per shot" and rank the over-budget offenders.<br>**USP:** Not generic infra monitor