e setlist schedule and autonomously flips quest-unlock flags at the right song moments.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** Replit Agent API builds the game; Replit Deployments serves the live URL fans open on their phones; Replit DB is the shared live state store for quest flags and scores; a scheduled/always-on Replit backend runs the Live-ops sub-agent that mutates DB flags on the setlist timeline mid-event.<br>**USP:** It's not a static generated app — the always-on agent performs *live-ops during the event*, turning a one-shot codegen into a running, reacting service tied to a real-world timeline.<br>**Demoability:** Show the deployed game on a phone, advance a mock setlist clock, and watch a locked quest pop open live for all connected players the instant the "next song" fires.<br><br>### 3. PlotBranch — *publishing / interactive serialized fiction*<br>**One-liner:** An agent that turns a writer's outline into a deployed, choose-your-path serialized webnovel where reader votes steer the next chapter the agent writes and ships nightly.<br>**M&E problem:** Digital publishers and serial-fiction platforms (webtoon/webnovel style) know branching interactive stories drive retention, but authoring branch trees, building the reader app, and running the nightly release cadence is manual and unsustainable per title.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini ingests the author's premise, characters, and tone bible and reasons out the branching story graph plus the current chapter. Agent Builder runs a Builder sub-agent (Agent API scaffolds the reader web app with accounts and a voting UI), a Deploy sub-agent, and an always-on Showrunner sub-agent that, on a nightly schedule, tallies the reader vote, has Gemini write the winning branch's next chapter consistent with the story-so-far state, and publishes it.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** Replit Agent API scaffolds the reader/voting app; Replit Auth gates reader accounts so votes are one-per-fan; Replit DB holds the canonical story graph, per-branch vote tallies, and each reader's traversal path as live state; a scheduled Replit backend fires the nightly Showrunner sub-agent that reads DB tallies and writes the new chapter; Deployments serves the evolving public URL.<br>**USP:** The story is a living deployed service whose canon is co-authored by reader votes and an autonomous nightly writer that maintains continuity from DB state — a self-running serialized product, not a static generator or a chatbot.<br>**Demoability:** Open the live URL, read chapter 3, cast a branch vote, fast-forward the nightly scheduler, and watch a brand-new chapter 4 (matching the winning vote and prior canon) appear on the deployed site.<br><br>### 4. SplitReel — *advertising / campaign performance optimization*<br>**One-liner:** An agent that autonomously generates, deploys, and continuously A/B-optimizes multiple live landing pages for a film or show trailer, killing losers and breeding winners on its own.<br>**M&E problem:** Trailer and campaign teams run paid traffic to promo pages but can't produce or test enough creative variants fast enough; underperforming pages keep burning ad spend because nobody has time to iterate mid-flight.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reasons over the campaign brief and audience to draft several distinct landing-page hypotheses (hook, CTA, layout, tone). Agent Builder runs a Builder sub-agent (Agent API scaffolds a multi-variant page app with click tracking), a Deploy sub-agent, and an always-on Optimizer sub-agent that reads live engagement metrics, has Gemini diagnose *why* a variant is winning/losing, then autonomously retires weak variants and prompts the Agent API to generate fresh challengers.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** Replit Agent API builds the variant pages and regenerates new ones each optimization round; Replit DB logs impressions/clicks/dwell per variant as the live metrics store and holds the traffic-split weights; a scheduled/always-on Replit backend runs the Optimizer that mutates split weights and triggers Agent API rebuilds; Deployments serves one stable public URL that internally routes to the current champion variants.<br>**USP:** It closes the full creative-optimization loop autonomously — generate → deploy → measure → Gemini-diagnose → regenerate — so the codegen agent behaves like a self-driving growth marketer rather than a one-time page builder.<br>**Demoability:** Show the live URL serving variant A, pump in simulated click data favoring variant B, run the Optimizer, and watch the agent explain its reasoning, kill variant A, spin up a new variant C, and reweight traffic on the deployed site.<br><br>### 5. DailiesDrop — *post-production / VFX review & approval*<br>**One-liner:** An agent that stands up a secure, deployed dailies review portal for a post team, with timecoded notes and a sub-agent that turns scattered feedback into a clean, actionable shot list.<br>**M&E problem:** Post-production and VFX review is fragmented across email, Slack, and expensive seat-licensed tools; supervisors drown in redundant, vague timecoded notes and manually collate them into shot fixes, delaying turnarounds.<br>**How the agent works:** Gemini reads the project's shot/reel structure and configures the portal's review flow. Agent Builder runs a Builder sub-agent (Agent API scaffolds a gated media-review app with a timecode-anchored commenting UI), a Deploy sub-agent, and a Notes-Synthesis sub-agent that reads all reviewer comments on a clip and has Gemini cluster, de-duplicate, and rank them into a prioritized per-shot fix list with severity, flagging contradictions between reviewers.<br>**Partner integration (the core):** Replit Agent API scaffolds the review portal; Replit Auth restricts the deployed site to invited reviewers/supervisors; Object Storage holds the uploaded dailies/shot media served in the player; Replit DB stores timecoded comments and the synthesized shot list as live state; Deployment